# bigcore.config -- Kconfig fragment for arm64 machines that are NOT SBCs.
#
# Merged on top of arch/arm64/configs/defconfig by the kernel's own fragment
# rule: `make defconfig bigcore.config` (scripts/kconfig/Makefile %.config).
# That defconfig is deliberately sized for the libre-computer SBC fleet, where
# four cores is typical and eight is the ceiling. It is not a bug that it stops
# at eight; it is the class it serves. This fragment is the other class.
#
# Who is in this class today:
#   ASUS Vivobook S15            Snapdragon X1E80100   12 cores   device-tree boot
#   NVIDIA DGX Spark             NVIDIA GB10           20 cores   UEFI/ACPI boot
#                                (10x Cortex-X925 + 10x Cortex-A725, 1 NUMA node)
#
# Note the two do NOT share a boot method, so this is not an "ACPI fragment".
# What they share is being large machines rather than boards, which is exactly
# the axis the SBC defconfig is sized against.
#
# Moved in-tree from infra/build/configs/arm64-bigcore.config on 2026-09-08 so
# that build.sh's linux.sh can name it on the config line; the symbol set is
# unchanged, byte for byte, and the resolved .config is verified identical by
# infra/build/kernel-funnel-check.sh.
#
# ── Sizing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The SBC defconfig sets CONFIG_NR_CPUS=8. On a 12-core X1E that silently loses
# four cores; on the 20-core Spark it loses twelve. Nothing warns you -- the
# machine boots and is simply smaller than it should be, which is the failure
# mode this fragment exists to prevent.
#
# ⚠ PROPOSED VALUE, not a looked-up fleet policy. There is no existing fleet
# value for this class, so per the "policy value is looked up, never invented"
# rule the basis is stated rather than assumed:
#   - 64 covers the largest big-core machine we run (20) with ample headroom,
#     and leaves room for a future socket without another config decision.
#   - Upstream's own arm64 default is 512 (arch/arm64/Kconfig, range 2-4096),
#     and Canonical's linux-nvidia-6.17 ships that default. 512 is sized for
#     arbitrary servers and costs per-CPU mask and array memory we have no
#     machine to justify.
# If the operator prefers matching upstream and the vendor, change this to 512;
# the tradeoff is memory, not function. Any value from 2 to 4096 is legal.
CONFIG_NR_CPUS=64

# ── Page size + IOMMU default ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Decision 2026-09-20: the whole bigcore
# class -- Snapdragon X1E80100 AND GB10 -- runs 64 KiB
# pages with the IOMMU default domain PASSTHROUGH. This overrides the SBC
# defconfig's IOMMU_DEFAULT_DMA_STRICT=y and its header note ("Do NOT
# reintroduce CONFIG_IOMMU_DEFAULT_PASSTHROUGH without X1E USB/HID regression
# test"): that regression test IS the first boot of this kernel on the X1E80100,
# and the class is not promoted onto linux-6.18.y-lc until it passes. The
# SBC fleet is untouched -- this is a class fragment, the base defconfig keeps
# 4K + DMA_STRICT (MESON_SM, the Amlogic secure monitor, is `depends on
# ARM64_4K_PAGES`; no Amlogic silicon is in this class).
#
# What 64K forces (arch/arm64/Kconfig, read 2026-09-20): ARM64_VA_BITS_48
# stays legal (3 page-table levels instead of 4); ARM64_PA_BITS_48 stays;
# ARM64_CONT_PTE_SHIFT/CONT_PMD_SHIFT become 5 (32 entries), so hugetlb sizes
# are 2M (cont-PTE) / 512M (PMD) / 16G (cont-PMD), PUD folded; MAX_ORDER
# becomes 13; COMPAT (32-bit EL0) is `depends on ARM64_4K_PAGES || EXPERT`
# and drops; THP_SWAP (ARCH_WANTS_THP_SWAP if ARM64_4K_PAGES) drops;
# ARM64_LPA2 is 4K/16K-only and was already n. The resolved-.config delta
# is recorded in the bigcore 64K-passthrough decision record.
#
# PASSTHROUGH removes the SMMU's DMA isolation for every device on both
# machines (a device DMA bug can scribble on any physical page); the GB10
# vendor kernel ships this default and boots iommu.passthrough=1 as well, and
# its arm-smmu-v3 iGPU quirk (on topic/lc618-spark) keeps the GPU in a DMA
# domain regardless.
#
# The choice symbols need both halves: merge_config.sh only takes a `# ... is
# not set` line as a request when the =y of its sibling is also present.
CONFIG_ARM64_64K_PAGES=y
# CONFIG_ARM64_4K_PAGES is not set
CONFIG_IOMMU_DEFAULT_PASSTHROUGH=y
# CONFIG_IOMMU_DEFAULT_DMA_STRICT is not set

# ── NVIDIA / Tegra ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Moved here from arch/arm64/configs/defconfig on 2026-09-07 (operator rule:
# NVIDIA belongs in this fragment, not in the shared SBC defconfig). The only
# NVIDIA silicon this fleet runs on arm64 is the DGX Spark's GB10 (T264 class),
# and it is in this class; no libre-computer SBC carries a Tegra, so every one of
# these symbols was dead weight in every SBC kernel -- built-in (=y) drivers
# included, which cost image size on boards that PXE-boot the image over TFTP.
#
# The set is the upstream arm64 defconfig's Tegra/NVIDIA selection, verbatim,
# including the older SoC generations (132/210/186/194/234/241): the GB10 port
# ports vendor drivers INTO the Tegra subsystems (pmc, gpio-tegra186, bpmp,
# i2c-tegra), and their Kconfig depends on ARCH_TEGRA, so ARCH_TEGRA=y is what
# makes the port compile at all. Trimming the generations the Spark does not
# have is a separate decision with its own .config comparison.
#
# Verified by .config comparison, not by grep (see the migration note below):
# the Spark's resolved .config is byte-identical before and after the move, and
# the SBC (amlogic) .config loses exactly these symbols and what only they
# select, nothing else.
CONFIG_ARCH_TEGRA=y
CONFIG_ARM_TEGRA186_CPUFREQ=y
CONFIG_PCI_TEGRA=y
CONFIG_PCIE_TEGRA194_HOST=m
CONFIG_PCIE_TEGRA194_EP=m
CONFIG_TEGRA_ACONNECT=m
CONFIG_TEGRA_BPMP=y
CONFIG_DWMAC_TEGRA=m
CONFIG_SERIAL_TEGRA=y
CONFIG_SERIAL_TEGRA_TCU=y
CONFIG_I2C_TEGRA=y
CONFIG_SPI_TEGRA210_QUAD=m
CONFIG_SPI_TEGRA114=m
CONFIG_TEGRA_SOCTHERM=m
CONFIG_TEGRA_BPMP_THERMAL=m
CONFIG_DRM_NOUVEAU=m
CONFIG_DRM_TEGRA=m
CONFIG_SND_HDA_TEGRA=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA210_AHUB=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA210_DMIC=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA210_I2S=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA210_OPE=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA186_ASRC=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA186_DSPK=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA210_ADMAIF=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA210_MVC=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA210_SFC=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA210_AMX=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA210_ADX=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA210_MIXER=m
CONFIG_SND_SOC_TEGRA_AUDIO_GRAPH_CARD=m
CONFIG_USB_XHCI_TEGRA=y
CONFIG_USB_TEGRA_XUDC=m
CONFIG_MMC_SDHCI_TEGRA=y
CONFIG_RTC_DRV_TEGRA=y
CONFIG_TEGRA186_GPC_DMA=y
CONFIG_TEGRA20_APB_DMA=y
CONFIG_TEGRA210_ADMA=m
CONFIG_TEGRA186_TIMER=y
CONFIG_TEGRA_HSP_MBOX=y
CONFIG_TEGRA_IOMMU_SMMU=y
CONFIG_ARCH_TEGRA_132_SOC=y
CONFIG_ARCH_TEGRA_210_SOC=y
CONFIG_ARCH_TEGRA_186_SOC=y
CONFIG_ARCH_TEGRA_194_SOC=y
CONFIG_ARCH_TEGRA_234_SOC=y
CONFIG_ARCH_TEGRA_241_SOC=y
CONFIG_ARCH_TEGRA_264_SOC=y
CONFIG_PWM_TEGRA=m
CONFIG_PHY_TEGRA_XUSB=y
CONFIG_NVIDIA_CORESIGHT_PMU_ARCH_SYSTEM_PMU=m
CONFIG_HTE_TEGRA194=y
CONFIG_HTE_TEGRA194_TEST=m
CONFIG_CRYPTO_DEV_TEGRA=m

# ── Wired NIC ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# BUILT-IN, not a module, for the Realtek NICs this driver does cover. A
# modular NIC cannot come up before modules are loadable, so a missing or
# mismatched initramfs makes the machine unreachable rather than merely
# degraded; =y removes that failure mode. boards.sh has asserted
# CONFIG_R8169=y for spark since before this fragment existed, and the class
# fragment is the layer that owns it now: the X1E80100 and the Spark share ONE bigcore
# kernel, so the symbol cannot be a per-board override.
#
# This IS the Spark's wired port. Measured 2026-09-26 on two DGX Spark
# units: `lspci -nn` reports 0007:01:00.0 as [10ec:8127], and 0x8127 is in
# r8169's own rtl8169_pci_tbl[] (r8169_main.c), matched as RTL_GIGA_MAC_VER_80
# "RTL8127A". So in-tree r8169 claims it; there is nothing left to port.
#
# `r8127` is the Realtek/NVIDIA OUT-OF-TREE vendor module, not a missing
# upstream driver -- the stock 7.0.0-nvidia kernel binds that device to r8127
# because it ships that module, while its own CONFIG_R8169=m sits unloaded.
# On linux-6.18.y-lc there is no r8127, and r8169 handles the part.
#
# Its PHY firmware (rtl_nic/rtl8127a-1.fw) is NOT in linux-firmware on the
# builder or on either Spark, and that is non-fatal: rtl_request_firmware()
# returns void and simply frees the request on failure, so probe succeeds and
# the NIC runs without the PHY firmware update.
#
# The shared arm64 defconfig carries =y too (it was =m). Both, deliberately:
# the fragment states the class's own requirement rather than inheriting a
# value the SBC defconfig is free to change, and the base gives every other
# arm64 machine the same built-in NIC. merge_config only ADDS, so a fragment
# line that the base already satisfies is a no-op -- but if the base ever goes
# back to =m, this line is what keeps the bigcore kernel bootable, and
# LB_LINUX_checkConfig's survival check fails loudly if it stops holding.
CONFIG_R8169=y

# ── Deliberately NOT set here ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# CONFIG_ACPI_NUMA: not settable and not needed. It is `def_bool NUMA && !X86`
#   in drivers/acpi/numa/Kconfig, and the base defconfig already sets
#   CONFIG_NUMA=y, so it is already y on every arm64 build. An earlier draft of
#   this fragment set it explicitly; that was noise.
#
# CONFIG_NODES_SHIFT: arm64 defaults to 4, i.e. 16 NUMA nodes. The vendor
#   kernel raises it to 6 for large multi-socket Grace servers. Neither machine
#   in this class is one, so the default stands.
#
# CONFIG_HZ_1000: the vendor kernel uses it and the base defconfig sets HZ_300.
#   It is a plausible change for an inference host, but no measurement here
#   justifies it and the base already sets CONFIG_PREEMPT=y. Inventing a tick
#   rate to match a vendor is exactly the guesswork the fragment is meant to
#   avoid. Revisit with a latency measurement, not a preference.
#
# CONFIG_R8127: RETIRED as a to-do 2026-09-26, and it was never a kernel
#   symbol -- `r8127` is Realtek's out-of-tree vendor module, which the stock
#   NVIDIA kernel ships and binds. The Spark's wired port is [10ec:8127]
#   (measured on two DGX Spark units), and upstream r8169 already lists
#   0x8127 as RTL8127A, so the part is supported in-tree. Nothing to port;
#   CONFIG_R8169=y above is what covers it.
#
# The ~164 CONFIG_*QCOM* symbols currently in the shared SBC defconfig arguably
#   belong in this class rather than in the base. That migration is deliberately
#   NOT done here, and it must not be attempted by name matching, because the
#   set is not name-derivable:
#     - CONFIG_QCOM_QMI_HELPERS is a bare `tristate` whose only dependency is
#       NET. It is auto-selected by ath10k_snoc / ath11k, which the shared
#       defconfig enables and which are not themselves ARCH_QCOM-gated. Removing
#       it by name is harmless only because it gets re-selected -- which is luck,
#       not design.
#     - CONFIG_ARCH_QCOM=y gates a large dependency tree (PCIE_QCOM, the GENI
#       serial console, pinctrl, cpufreq). Dropping it from the base is not a
#       line deletion, it is a subtree deletion.
#   So the migration is a .config COMPARISON, not a grep: build each platform
#   before and after, and require the resulting .config files to be identical.
#   Anything else silently changes SBC kernels.
